  "query_preview": "..\n\nA trough running along the Canadian border will send a Canadian \nsurface front through the forecast area and will bring a northerly \nwind shift and a brief cooldown tonight into Monday. Moisture \nwithin the westerly flow aloft will maintain variable middle and\nhigher level cloudiness and perhaps a few spotty light showers. \nRidging aloft strengthens and brings a return to hot conditions \nheading towards the middle of the week. Emebedded shortwaves \nalong the ridge are expected to interact with the monsoonal \nmoisture leading to daily chances for showers and thunderstorms \nacross the region, especially in Southwest Montana. The main \nconcern, especially for Wednesday, will be an elevated chance for \nheavy rainfall which may lead to localized flash flooding.\n\nTowards the weekend, the ridge axis moves eastward allowing for \ndrier air to filter in and temperatures to warm across the region. \nThere are hints in the models that this ridge could break down \ntowards the weekend which would help moderate some of the currently \nforecasted highs but it would come at the price of windier \nconditions which may still lead to increase fire weather concerns.\n-thor",
